.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt RECVMSG 2
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm recv ,
.Nm recvfrom ,
.Nm recvmsg
.Nd receive a message from a socket
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn recv "int s" "void *buf" "size_t len" "int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fo recvfrom
.Fa "int s"
.Fa "void *buf"
.Fa "size_t len"
.Fa "int flags"
.Fa "struct sockaddr *from"
.Fa "socklen_t *fromlen"
.Fc
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn recvmsg "int s" "struct msghdr *msg" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls receive data from the socket
.Fa s .
The
.Fn recvmsg
system call does the work; the C library implements
.Fn recv
and
.Fn recvfrom
over it, as the native system-call interface passes at most four
arguments.
.Pp
.Fn recv
receives at most
.Fa len
bytes into
.Fa buf .
.Fn recvfrom
does the same and, if
.Fa from
is not
.Dv NULL ,
stores the sender's address there, at most
.Fa *fromlen
bytes of it, and sets
.Fa *fromlen
to the address's full length.
.Pp
.Fn recvmsg
uses a
.Vt struct msghdr ,
described in
.Xr sendmsg 2 .
The data is scattered into the
.Fa msg_iovlen
buffers of
.Fa msg_iov ,
at most
.Dv IOV_MAX
(1024) of them.
If
.Fa msg_name
is not
.Dv NULL ,
the sender's address is stored there, at most
.Fa msg_namelen
bytes of it, and
.Fa msg_namelen
is set to its full length, or to 0 if the message has no sender address
(on a connection, for example).
If
.Fa msg_control
is not
.Dv NULL ,
control data is stored there, at most
.Fa msg_controllen
bytes of it, and
.Fa msg_controllen
is set to the length stored.
Only local sockets produce control data; see
.Xr unix 4 .
.Fa msg_flags
is set to the OR of:
.Bl -tag -width MSG_CTRUNC
.It Dv MSG_TRUNC
The datagram or record was longer than the buffers given; the rest of it
has been discarded.
.It Dv MSG_CTRUNC
Some control data did not fit in
.Fa msg_control ,
or there was control data and
.Fa msg_control
is
.Dv NULL .
.It Dv MSG_EOR
The data ends a record
.Pq Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET No only .
.El
.Pp
On a datagram or sequenced-packet socket each call receives one message.
On a stream socket it receives what is there, up to the size of the
buffers, and never more than 1 MiB; bytes sent with control data are never
returned together with bytes sent before them.
A receive of 0 bytes on a connection is end-of-file.
With nothing to receive, the call blocks, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
if the socket is non-blocking.
A blocking receive waits at most as long as the socket's
.Dv SO_RCVTIMEO
option says, if it is set.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
are the OR of none or more of:
.Bl -tag -width MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC
.It Dv MSG_PEEK
Return the data without removing it: the next receive returns it again.
Descriptors sent with it stay in the message; credentials are shown.
.It Dv MSG_WAITALL
On a stream socket, keep receiving until the buffers are full, or
end-of-file, an error, a signal, a timeout, or bytes that were sent with
control data.
It has no effect on other sockets or with
.Dv MSG_PEEK .
.It Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
Do not block: fail with
.Er EAGAIN
instead, as if the socket had
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
.It Dv MSG_TRUNC
On a datagram or sequenced-packet socket, return the full length of the
message even if it was longer than the buffers given, as Linux does.
.It Dv MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC
Set the close-on-exec flag on descriptors received with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS .
.El
.Pp
Any other flag, including
.Dv MSG_OOB ,
makes the call fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
.Xr read 2
on a socket is
.Fn recvmsg
without an address, control data or flags; descriptors sent with the data
it returns are closed.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These calls return the number of bytes received, or the message's full
length with
.Dv MSG_TRUNC .
Otherwise they return \-1 and set
.Va errno .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The socket is non-blocking or
.Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
was given, and nothing is waiting; or
.Dv SO_RCVTIMEO
expired first.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa s
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er ECONNREFUSED , ECONNRESET , ETIMEDOUT
A TCP connection failed; the error is reported once.
.It Bq Er ECONNRESET
A local connection was reset: its listener closed before accepting it.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the call before anything was received.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa msg
is
.Dv NULL ,
.Fa msg_controllen
is more than 4096, an
.Fa iov_len
is too large, or a buffer pointer is
.Dv NULL
with a length that is not zero.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
.Fa msg_iovlen
is more than
.Dv IOV_MAX .
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
The socket is a stream or sequenced-packet socket that is not connected.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa s
is not a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
A flag other than those above was given.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr sendmsg 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr unix 4
.Sh STANDARDS
These calls conform to
.St -p1003.1-2008 .
.Dv MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC
is in IEEE Std 1003.1-2024.
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn recv ,
.Fn recvfrom
and
.Fn recvmsg
system calls first appeared in
.Bx 4.2 .
In OxideBSD
.Fn recv
and
.Fn recvfrom
are library functions over
.Fn recvmsg ,
system call 578; the former
.Fn recvfrom
system call, 143, is retired and fails with
.Er ENOSYS .
